A||||9||KissMySSFiero||T-top install||||1||
Z||000000||KissMySSFiero||11-25-2000||06:45 PM||||I was wondering how much is involved in installing t-tops on my car.  I found a doner car with the parts i think that i need. would it be best to cut my roof or use the roof from the doner car.  what kind of supports would I need.  what are the chances of it not leaking.  any advise is appreciated.  TIA<BR>JOEJOE||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||11-25-2000||07:25 PM||||JOEJOE,<P>I also found a donor car is the bone yard. The car's T-Top was complete and in OK shape. I used a 14" gas powered saw and cut the whole roof off. Before I started, I carefully removed the weather stripping and glass tops. Then, I just cut the four metal corners and the windshield. The back window just popped. I haven't installed it. I just wanted to let you know I was in the same boat.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000002||KissMySSFiero||11-25-2000||08:41 PM||||where is seffner, fl?  im in jax.||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||11-25-2000||09:32 PM||||Installing T-tops in a Fiero is a unique task due to the complexity of the space frame. The frame isnt a few solid pieces, but some 200+ (Ogre...the exact #?) pieces that are overlaped in different ways to make the unique frame. Cutting it is a task I'd leave to a pro, like Ed Parks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||11-25-2000||10:25 PM||||Seffner is near Tampa.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||11-25-2000||11:00 PM||||thanx for the help on the space frame info.  even though thats not what i wanted to hear.  damn i love those ttops.  is there anyone on the list who has successfully done this.  and how much do you think it would cost giving that i have theparts available?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||11-26-2000||08:18 AM||||I'm guessing labor will run anywhere between $500 and $800 if you have all of the necessary pieces.  Try either The Fiero Shop (Ed Parks in Toney, AL--closer to you) or Bob Stieger @ Twin Lakes Fiero (in ILL).  AFAIK, both of them have successfully installed Ttops.||||reg||1
Z||000007||pollock||11-26-2000||08:57 PM||||I have done this, although mine is not yet complete.  <P>Cutting it is not really that hard, and the fact that the space frame is made of a bunch of different pieces doesn't make that much difference to a sawzall.<P>I'd say chances are pretty good that it will leak.  Even if you just buy a fiero that already has em.<P>Right now i'm fighting a losing battle with the "fit and finish" of the tops.  The glass sits about 1/4-3/8 below the front trim piece, and i can't see any easy way to remedy that. The parts i have are not in the best of shape, and replacements are high dollar.<P>I don't quite regret it, not yet.  But if you want t-tops so bad, and believe me, i did too, there are easier ways to get them.  For instance, go buy a camaro.||||reg||2
Z||000008||JohnnyK||03-15-2001||09:46 PM||||**** . I just bought an 84 with t-tops in hopes that somehow I could remove the whole top of the car and replace it with the other one? This isn't possible?! I have to actually cut the metal? I thought the whole fiberglass roof could come off? Damn!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||03-15-2001||10:03 PM||||I have an 88 TTop and just looking at it, it doesn't look like a swap to a non TTop car is a job for the faint of heart. Johnny it is probably easier for you to swap the stuff from your other car to this 84. BTW did you check the VIN to see if it really is an 84?||||reg||1
